{"id":"n64-nso","name":"N64 Controller (Nintendo Switch Online)","vendor":"Nintendo","vid":"0x057E","pid":"0x2019","productString":"N64 Controller","manufacturerString":"Nintendo Co., Ltd.","type":"gamepad","connection":"bluetooth","bluetoothTransport":"classic","versionNumber":0,"descriptor":"","inputReportSize":362,"notes":"The 170-byte Bluetooth HID descriptor every Switch Bluetooth pad serves, captured from N64 controllers for Nintendo Switch Online (darthcloud bt_traces sw_pair.log and steam_win10_sw_2x_pairing.pcapng, Nefarius.Utilities.Bluetooth Dumps). The pad's behavior is this profile's data, on switch-pro's mechanisms. Before a host speaks the protocol it sends the 12-byte 0x3F report with the stick on X and Y, Rx at 0x8000 and Ry at 0x5C6F, the constant two captured units send, the D-pad as the hat, and B, A, C-Up, C-Left, L, R, Z, C-Down, C-Right, Start, ZR, Home and Capture as buttons 1 to 11, 13 and 14 (BlueRetro sw.c, hid-nintendo.c). Its device info is a real pad's reply (darthcloud bt_traces switch_assert_after_sniff_mode.txt, unit 20:0B:CF:17:64:36): firmware 04 11, device type 0C, then 02, the address, and 00 02. The stick parameters at 0x6086 are the captured pad's, and the stick calibration is the profile's own, centered on the values the state block encodes. No public source gives its colors or six-axis block. After the handshake the 0x30 report carries C-Up on the Y bit, C-Left on X, C-Down on ZR, C-Right on Minus, Z on ZL, ZR on the left stick click and Start on Plus (hid-nintendo.c n64con_button_mappings), the stick in the left stick bytes, 80 in byte 2 and 08 in byte 12, as the fixture shows. The pad has one motor. Rumble is the left half of output 0x01 or 0x10, raised on OutputDecoded as leftMotor: hosts write both halves alike, and no source says which half the motor reads. On the USB/IP backend the device presents this same Bluetooth form, and its first compatible ID is a BTHENUM marker, which SDL's hidapi reads as the Bluetooth bus.","